last night at the 73rd Tony Awards in  New York City it wasn't only theater  being celebrated but pride as well and  the stars who walked the red carpet were  more than happy to share their support  for the LGBTQ community community  historically have excluded no one race  gender sexuality they open their arms to  everybody and historically always have  the Tony Awards have always shared the  message of inclusivity but this year it  couldn't be missed with the rainbow  floral wall which lined the red carpet  well it's beautiful I think it's so  wonderful that you can celebrate  celebrate that and and and make that  statement as an award show as a network  of it to be able to make that statement  to do it in a way that everybody feels  equal and proud is a wonderful thing I'm  so I'm very proud to support it  actress and activist Judith Light knows  that the Tony Awards and pride have  always gone hand in hand but this year  it is especially significant all the  things that have come out of this  community in support of the issues and  the lgbtq+ community and the HIV and  AIDS community they have been the ones  to step up and do something this doesn't  surprise me  I'm just thrilled that it's tonight and  fifty years of pride for the first time  world Pride will be hosted in the United  States in honor of the 50th anniversary  of Stonewall and its massive  significance in LGBTQ rights actor Bryan  Cranston shared the simple reminder of  opening ourselves up to differences we  can't live in a capsule we can't live in  our own little bubble we have to  spread the message of equality and love  and acceptance and in embracing  differences everywhere we go and  although we are seeing pride nearly  everywhere this month dr. Zachary Quinto  reminds us that there's still work to be  done well certainly we've made  tremendous strides in certain ways and  then in other ways we have a lot of work  to do but that's always the case with  social movements so marriage equality  you know doing away with Don't Ask Don't  Tell there have been incredible  political and social advances but  obviously we're living in a time when  there's an administration and a faction  of politics in this country would you  like to see all of that roll back not  only for the LGBT community but also for  women and minorities of all kinds so I  think we have to stay vigilant we have  to stay active we have to stay engaged  and visibility is a huge part of that so  what are your plans for Pride Month how  are you gonna celebrate and show your  pride what do you think of the rainbow  floral Tony Awards red carpet let me  know in the comments below and don't  forget to subscribe to ET Canada's  YouTube page  you
 

